<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Does Using Counterfactual Help LLMs Explain 
<br class="ltx_break">Textual Importance in Classification?</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Nelvin Tan
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>American Express
<span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Correspondence:</span><a href="mailto:thongcainelvin.tan@aexp.com" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">thongcainelvin.tan@aexp.com</a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">James Asikin Cheung
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>American Express
<span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Correspondence:</span><a href="mailto:thongcainelvin.tan@aexp.com" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">thongcainelvin.tan@aexp.com</a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yu-Ching Shih
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>American Express
<span id="id3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Correspondence:</span><a href="mailto:thongcainelvin.tan@aexp.com" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">thongcainelvin.tan@aexp.com</a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Dong Yang
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>American Express
<span id="id4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Correspondence:</span><a href="mailto:thongcainelvin.tan@aexp.com" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">thongcainelvin.tan@aexp.com</a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Amol Salunkhe
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>American Express
<span id="id5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Correspondence:</span><a href="mailto:thongcainelvin.tan@aexp.com" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">thongcainelvin.tan@aexp.com</a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs) are becoming useful in many domains due to their impressive abilities that arise from large training datasets and large model sizes. More recently, they have been shown to be very effective in textual classification tasks, motivating the need to explain the LLMs’ decisions. Motivated by practical constrains where LLMs are black-boxed and LLM calls are expensive, we study how incorporating counterfactuals into LLM reasoning can affect the LLM’s ability to identify the top words that have contributed to its classification decision. To this end, we introduce a framework called the <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">decision changing rate</span> that helps us quantify the importance of the top words in classification. Our experimental results show that using counterfactuals can be helpful.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_align_bottom">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center" style="width:345.0pt;"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs) are becoming useful in many domains <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2023a)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sakai and Lam (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tan et al. (2025)</a></cite> due to their impressive abilities that arise from large training datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2023b)</a></cite> and large model sizes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Naveed et al. (2023)</a></cite>. More recently, they have been shown to be very effective in textual classification tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al. (2024b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sakai and Lam (2025)</a></cite>, motivating the need to explain the LLMs’ decisions. In this paper, we investigate how well LLMs can explain their decisions when making textual classifications – specifically, we study the LLM’s ability to select the top <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> key words that led to its classification decision. Some of the potential business use cases of being able to identify the keys words that lead to a certain LLM classification are as follows: (i) Can aid the prompt designer to trim unneccesary words or shorten phrases. This reduces the number of tokens processed which helps to reduce cost. (ii) Helps to provide auditable records of how text inputs drive LLM classifications.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Conterfactuals.</h4>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we focus on a specific type of self-generated explanations called self-generated counterfactual explanations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wachter et al. (2017)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dehghanighobadi et al. (2025)</a></cite> – which we call simply as counterfactuals: Given an input <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and the LLM output <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math>, the counterfactual <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a minimally modified version (i.e., the edit/Levenshtein distance between <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are minimized) of the input such that when <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is feed into the LLM, its output <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}\neq\hat{y}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>≠</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}\neq\hat{y}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. For example, <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">‘This item is neither affordable, nor of good quality.’</span> and <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">‘This item is both affordable, and of good quality.’</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Note that we do not solve any optimization problem to obtain <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, but simply ask for the LLM for <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is motivated by recent work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dehghanighobadi et al. (2025)</a></cite> that showed that LLMs are generally able to produce counterfactuals with a high success rate for classification problems with a few classes. We build on their work to study how these counterfactuals affect key-word identificiation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Problem setup.</h4>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given an input <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and completely black-box LLM (i.e., no accesss to weights, gradients, etc.), we want to produce the top-<math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> words that explain the classification decision of the LLM, where <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is up to the user to choose.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Motivation.</h4>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our problem setup is motivated by businesses that use black-box LLMs (e.g., GPT-4o) but have no access to the inner workings of the LLMs. At the same time, LLM calls are expensive so we want to minimize it for cost savings. This leads to incoporating counterfactuals into LLM reasoning as a viable approach for LLM explainability. Our main research questions are:</p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Do counterfactuals help LLM with explaining the importance of words in textual classification?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">How do we score the LLM’s ability to select the top-<math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> words that influenced its classification decision?</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Known black-box explainability techniques for textual classification and for LLMs are as follows:</p>
<ul id="S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">SHAP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lundberg and Lee (2017)</a></cite> and LIME <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ribeiro et al. (2016)</a></cite> are common input-feature based techniques that works reasonably well for smaller models, but are they too expensive for LLMs, requiring 100-1000 LLM calls per classification output which is not practical due to the high cost. Furthermore, prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ichikawa and Steup (2014)</a></cite> argues that counterfactuals constitute a better test of knowledge over LIME and SHAP.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prompting the LLM directly can take as low as 1 prompt, but this totally ignores the internal mechanism of the LLM and it has been shown that LLMs do not always say what they think <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2025)</a></cite>. Nevertheless, it has been shown to align with human intuition <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Agarwal et al. (2024)</a></cite>. There has also been other work that has argued that the contrastive nature of counterfactuals are better aligned with human reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Miller (2019)</a></cite> – making it interesting to study whether counterfactuals can improve on direct prompting.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Black-box approaches aside, there are many other LLM explainability approaches – we refer the interested reader to the following surveys <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al. (2024a)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Luo and Specia (2024)</a></cite> for a comprehensive review.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Main contributions.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With respect to the main research questions, are contributions are as follows:</p>
<ol id="S2.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We investigate the usefuless of counterfactuals in helping LLM explain the importance of input words in textual classification. Our experimental results show that using counterfactuals can help improve the identification of the top-<math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> words.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To achieve the above goal, we introduce a metric known as the <span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">decision-changing</span> rate, which captures how important the top-<math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> words to the LLM’s classification decision. To the best of our knowledge, this is a new metric that could be useful for the field.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Methodology</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce 3 approaches to obtain the top-<math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> words that led to an LLM’s classification decision: <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">direct prompting</span>, <span id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">counterfactual-parallel</span>, and <span id="S3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">counterfactual-sequential</span>. The prompts for all 3 approaches are given in Appendices <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Prompt for Amazon Dataset ‣ Does Using Counterfactual Help LLMs Explain Textual Importance in Classification?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>, <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Prompt for SST2 Dataset ‣ Does Using Counterfactual Help LLMs Explain Textual Importance in Classification?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>, and <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Prompt for IMDB Dataset ‣ Does Using Counterfactual Help LLMs Explain Textual Importance in Classification?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>, and we give the high-level ideas below.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Direct prompting (DP).</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Directly prompt the LLM for the classification and ask it to list the top-<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> most important words in the input that led to its classification decision. This is shown visually in Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Counterfactual-sequential (CFS). ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Does Using Counterfactual Help LLMs Explain Textual Importance in Classification?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Counterfactual-parallel (CFP).</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The key steps are shown visually in Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Counterfactual-sequential (CFS). ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Does Using Counterfactual Help LLMs Explain Textual Importance in Classification?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and are stated as follows: (1.) Ask the LLM to produce the counterfactual <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. (2.) Check if the LLM outputs satisfy <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}^{\prime}\neq\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≠</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}^{\prime}\neq\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math>; if this is not true then use DP to produce the top-<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> most important words. Otherwise, proceed with the next step. (3. Parallel step) Feed <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> into the LLM and ask it to produce the top-<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> most important words that led to its previous classification decision <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Counterfactual-sequential (CFS).</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The key steps are shown visually in Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Counterfactual-sequential (CFS). ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Does Using Counterfactual Help LLMs Explain Textual Importance in Classification?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and are stated as follows: (1.) Use DP to get the top-<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> most important words that led to its classification decision <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math>. (2.) Ask the LLM to produce the counterfactual <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. (3.) Check if the LLM outputs satisfy <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}^{\prime}\neq\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≠</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}^{\prime}\neq\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math>; if this is not true then use DP to produce the top-<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> most important words. Otherwise, proceed with the next step. (4. Refinement step) Feed <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> into the LLM and ask it to refine its top-<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> most important words that led to its classification decision. It is allowed to keep the initial top <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> words as the final choice of words if it is confident.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.04031v1/images/approaches.png" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/209;" width="476" height="209" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Visualization of the different approaches</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Extensions to CFP and CFS.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We can take advantage of multiple LLM prompts (i.e., sampling) to produce a weights vector corresponding to the words in the input text, where each word in the input text corresponds to a weight and the weight indicates how important the word is in the classification decision. The key steps are as follows: (i) Set the temperature of the LLM to be greater than 0 (e.g., 1), and decide on the number of important words <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. (ii) Have a vector of weights <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{w}</annotation></semantics></math> (initialized to all zeros) with each entry corresponding to a word in <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}</annotation></semantics></math>. (iii) Run either CFP or CFS <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> times. For each run, if a word is included in the top-<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> most important words, then increment its corresponding weight in <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{w}</annotation></semantics></math> by 1. (iv) Divide all entries in <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{w}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and produce <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{w}</annotation></semantics></math> as the final output. This vector <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{w}</annotation></semantics></math> can be used to produce a heatmap of the words.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Dataset and Evaluation Metric</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We look at the following textual binary classification datasets of different lengths:</p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Amazon <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kotzias et al. (2015)</a></cite> (short-length).</span> Dataset consists of 500 positive and 500 negative sentences from customer reviews were extracted from the <a href="https://amazon.com" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">amazon.com</a>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SST2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pang and Lee (2005)</a></cite> (medium-length).</span> Dataset consists of 11,855 single sentences extracted from movie reviews.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">IMDB <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Maas et al. (2011)</a></cite> (long-length).</span> Dataset consists of 50,000 reviews from the Internet Movie Database (IMDb) labeled as positive or negative.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We use the following LLMs with temperature set to 0 for consistency of results: LLaMA3-70B (weaker model) and GPT-4o (stronger model). For each dataset, we randomly sample 100 reviews for our experiments – for our IMDB dataset, whenever the review exceeds the context length of LLaMA3-70B, we manually modify it to fit into the context length (we also run GPT-4o on this modified dataset). Details of our sampled dataset are as follows:</p>
<ul id="S4.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Amazon.</span> Average of 10.86 words per review; our aim would be to seek the top-<math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> most important words, where <math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1,2,3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1,2,3</annotation></semantics></math> (9.21%, 18.42%, 27.62% correspondingly).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SST2.</span> Average of 17.76 words per review; our aim would be to seek the top-<math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> most important words, where <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1,2,3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1,2,3</annotation></semantics></math> (5.63%, 11.26%, 16.89% correspondingly).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">IMDB.</span> Average of 213.28 words per review; our aim would be to seek the top-<math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> most important words, where <math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=3,5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=3,5</annotation></semantics></math> (1.41%, 2.34% correspondingly).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">We run the following approaches: CFP, CFS, and DP. For each approach, the same LLM is used throughout the entire approach. The prompts for the experiments are given in Appendices <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Prompt for Amazon Dataset ‣ Does Using Counterfactual Help LLMs Explain Textual Importance in Classification?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> (Amazon dataset), <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Prompt for SST2 Dataset ‣ Does Using Counterfactual Help LLMs Explain Textual Importance in Classification?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> (SST2 dataset), and <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Prompt for IMDB Dataset ‣ Does Using Counterfactual Help LLMs Explain Textual Importance in Classification?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> (IMDB dataset).</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Decision changing rate.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce a metric to measure explainability: <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">decision-changing</span> rate (DCR), which is the average of the decision-changing scores defined below:</p>
<ul id="S4.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For a given list of important words <math id="S4.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> and input text <math id="S4.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, for each word <math id="S4.I3.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\in\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\in\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, replace it in <math id="S4.I3.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}</annotation></semantics></math> with ‘{MASK}’.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Ask the LLM (that initially gave the classification), to produce a modified review <math id="S4.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> by replacing each sub-string ‘{MASK}’ with a word or short phrase such that the overall sentiment of the movie review gets flipped. The LLM is also asked to make sure that no other replacements are made, and that the review flows coherently.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Feed <math id="S4.I3.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> back into the LLM and ask for its classification. If the classification is flipped, then we set the decision-changing score to be 1. Otherwise, we set it to be 0.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Main Results</h3>

<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S4.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t" rowspan="2"><span id="S4.T1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Approach</span></th>
<th id="S4.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t" colspan="4"><span id="S4.T1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Amazon (short)</span></th>
<th id="S4.T1.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t" colspan="4"><span id="S4.T1.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SST2 (medium)</span></th>
<th id="S4.T1.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t" colspan="2"><span id="S4.T1.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">IMDB (long)</span></th>
<td id="S4.T1.2.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_border_t"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column">Acc.</th>
<th id="S4.T1.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column"><math id="S4.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{DCR}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>DCR</mtext><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{DCR}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S4.T1.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column"><math id="S4.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{DCR}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>DCR</mtext><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{DCR}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S4.T1.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column"><math id="S4.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{DCR}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>DCR</mtext><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{DCR}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S4.T1.2.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column">Acc.</th>
